1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:01,860
So imagine walking into a cabinet meeting.

2
00:00:02,560 --> 00:00:09,080
You, uh, you slide this beautifully designed four-layer reference architecture diagram right across the table.

3
00:00:09,260 --> 00:00:12,220
Yeah. And, and you just watch your minister's eyes

4
00:00:12,860 --> 00:00:14,340
completely glaze over.

5
00:00:14,460 --> 00:00:16,720
Oh, yeah. We've all seen that exact look.

6
00:00:17,020 --> 00:00:17,220
Right.

7
00:00:18,440 --> 00:00:23,950
Today's deep dive is for you, the government official running these major systems.

8
00:00:24,810 --> 00:00:31,120
We are pulling insights from digital government playbooks to answer one really critical question.

9
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:36,740
How do you actually build a business case that gets your minister to say yes to the reference architecture?

10
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:40,880
Because the painful truth here is that ministers, well, they do not buy diagrams.

11
00:00:40,920 --> 00:00:41,600
No, they definitely do not.

12
00:00:41,740 --> 00:00:45,320
Right. They buy numbers, and they buy cases they can actually defend in cabinet.

13
00:00:45,380 --> 00:00:50,540
Exactly. So a brilliant technical solution by itself, I mean, it just won't secure the mandate or the funding.

14
00:00:50,730 --> 00:00:59,040
No, not at all. You have to start by illustrating the severe financial consequence of how things currently operate because right now government systems are highly fragmented.

15
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:07,280
Very fragmented. Yeah. Every time a program in your ministry builds its own identity system or, you know, its own registers, the data is siloed.

16
00:01:07,320 --> 00:01:09,140
And probably often rekeyed manually, right?

17
00:01:09,180 --> 00:01:17,960
Oh, constantly rekeyed. And you remain locked into one supplier per project, which basically makes your entire infrastructure incredibly costly to change.

18
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:25,300
So right now we are essentially letting every single neighborhood build its own bespoke power plant, to use a metaphor.

19
00:01:26,040 --> 00:01:29,920
But you are trying to sell the minister on building a national grid.

20
00:01:30,160 --> 00:01:32,500
That is exactly the dynamic at play, yes.

21
00:01:32,560 --> 00:01:35,220
The reference architecture is really just the how.

22
00:01:35,280 --> 00:01:38,560
What you are actually selling to the cabinet is the saving.

23
00:01:38,640 --> 00:01:40,170
The saving. Okay, tell me more about that.

24
00:01:40,650 --> 00:01:50,100
Well, by consuming shared building blocks instead of duplicating effort across different departments, you save a meaningful share of the sectoral digital budget over a five-year period.

25
00:01:50,180 --> 00:01:51,720
Wow, okay. That's a big deal.

26
00:01:51,880 --> 00:01:55,790
Yeah. You show them the immediate reduction in duplicated vendor licensing fees

27
00:01:56,400 --> 00:01:59,080
and, uh, redundant maintenance contracts.

28
00:01:59,100 --> 00:02:01,290
But hold on a second. If I am a minister,

29
00:02:01,960 --> 00:02:06,420
I don't think I care about sectoral digital budgets as much as I care about the front page headlines.

30
00:02:06,670 --> 00:02:07,980
That's a very fair point.

31
00:02:08,060 --> 00:02:15,940
Like, if you promise me massive savings, but this new unified system crashes on day one, my political career is just over.

32
00:02:15,950 --> 00:02:19,210
How do you get past that inherent political terror?

33
00:02:19,260 --> 00:02:22,480
Because any untested approach is a massive risk to them.

34
00:02:22,640 --> 00:02:26,560
Right. And that is exactly why you do not ask them to bet on a theory.

35
00:02:26,660 --> 00:02:28,240
You hand them a political shield.

36
00:02:28,380 --> 00:02:29,960
A political shield. I like that.

37
00:02:29,980 --> 00:02:33,180
Yeah. Yeah. You must back those projected savings with actual proof.

38
00:02:33,780 --> 00:02:40,180
You map out a charted path by demonstrating that other governments have successfully built this exact same pattern.

39
00:02:40,260 --> 00:02:42,750
Oh, so showing them that the obstacles are already documented.

40
00:02:42,829 --> 00:02:46,820
Documented and solved by people who have walked this exact path before you.

41
00:02:46,900 --> 00:02:50,520
That completely removes the political risk of being the first one to try it.

42
00:02:50,530 --> 00:02:51,500
That makes total sense.

43
00:02:52,260 --> 00:02:56,960
You show them the proof of success elsewhere, which I guess leads directly to the next part, which is the ask.

44
00:02:57,040 --> 00:03:06,720
Exactly. The ask. To execute this, you are telling the minister you need a small permanent team, a board with real authority, and about two percent of the

45
00:03:06,740 --> 00:03:08,980
digital budget sustained for those five years.

46
00:03:09,020 --> 00:03:11,860
Wait, why specifically two percent? That seems like a very specific number.

47
00:03:12,160 --> 00:03:13,500
Well, if you ask for ten percent,

48
00:03:14,180 --> 00:03:16,840
that triggers an immediate heavy-handed treasury audit.

49
00:03:16,920 --> 00:03:18,380
Oh, right. Of course it does.

50
00:03:18,600 --> 00:03:26,150
But two percent is small enough to fly under the radar of major fiscal cuts, yet it's large enough to fund a dedicated team of experts.

51
00:03:26,760 --> 00:03:30,650
Because without that sustained funding, your project gets starved.

52
00:03:31,300 --> 00:03:35,520
And I'm guessing your personnel just get poached by other day-to-day government crises.

53
00:03:35,580 --> 00:03:38,720
Exactly. They get pulled into whatever fire is burning that week.

54
00:03:39,420 --> 00:03:41,460
So that brings up the timeline, which is always tricky.

55
00:03:41,620 --> 00:03:47,260
Usually government IT projects, you know, they promise the moon by next quarter just to get the initial green light.

56
00:03:47,400 --> 00:03:51,300
And presenting an overly optimistic timeline like that is a total trap.

57
00:03:51,600 --> 00:03:52,440
Yeah, I can see that.

58
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:01,740
You have to offer the honest horizon. The real timeline for this kind of transformation is about six months to deliver a first roadmap that the minister can publicly show.

59
00:04:02,060 --> 00:04:03,620
Okay. Six months for a roadmap.

60
00:04:04,300 --> 00:04:06,460
Right. But it takes years to reach full maturity.

61
00:04:06,760 --> 00:04:11,220
Wait, you are telling a minister that they likely won't even be in office to see the project completed?

62
00:04:11,520 --> 00:04:14,500
Mm. I would think that immediately sinks the whole business case.

63
00:04:14,540 --> 00:04:17,840
Well, if it's framed as a weakness, it absolutely does sink the case.

64
00:04:18,360 --> 00:04:25,500
But if it's presented as the reality of serious institution building, it completely shifts the dynamic in your favor.

65
00:04:25,520 --> 00:04:31,430
How so? It separates a highly credible proposal from, say, a desperate salesman's pitch.

66
00:04:31,969 --> 00:04:35,670
Ministers will actually fund leaders who tell them the truth about the timeline.

67
00:04:35,880 --> 00:04:39,510
Because it proves you understand the actual gravity and scale of the undertaking.

68
00:04:39,510 --> 00:04:41,660
Exactly. It shows you're taking it seriously.

69
00:04:41,760 --> 00:04:46,840
So it all comes together as this ultimate one-page business case, and the order is very strict, right?

70
00:04:46,920 --> 00:04:48,120
First, the saving.

71
00:04:48,740 --> 00:04:49,580
Second, the proof.

72
00:04:49,840 --> 00:04:53,240
Third, the ask. Mm. And fourth, the honest horizon.

73
00:04:53,640 --> 00:05:02,240
Right. Bringing those four elements in that precise order turns a technical diagram into a highly defensible reality that they can actually champion in cabinet.

